  "worked_solution": "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,3],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,3]$ and differentiable on $(-1,3).$\n\nFirst, notice that the function $h(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,3]$ and differentiable on $(-1,3),$ so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nLet's check each of the other functions individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1.$ Hence, $f(x)$ is not continuous on $[-1,3].$ Therefore, $f(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $p(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,3]$ but has a corner point at $x=2.$ Hence, $p(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=2,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,3).$ Therefore, $p(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $r(x)$  has a vertical asymptote at $x=-1.$ Hence, $r(x)$  is not continuous on $[-1,3].$ Therefore, $r(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $t(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,3]$ but has a corner point at $x=0.$ Hence, $t(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=0,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,3).$ Therefore, $t(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.",
